A||N||8||polomeno||Help with neutral start switch please!!||||1||
Z||000000||polomeno||11-08-2000||07:00 PM||||Hey guys.. ever since i changed my clutch pedal my car wont start , I assume it is the neutral start switch that is causeing it cause i had the starter and battery changed a few weeks ago and it only started happening after i changed clutch pedal.. when i turn the key i get no power at all .. no click from the back.. and the temp gauge moves back and forth.. the switch seems to be workin correctly and is connected.. i tried taking the plug outta the switch and connecting the 2 prongs together to jump it.. i still get the same thing.. can anyone give me any idea's on what to do or check? is there a fuse for this or something? anything?<P>thanks<P>John||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||11-08-2000||11:12 PM||||Always trying to <B>start</B> something, aren't you  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">?<P>No idea what the problem is.  Guess I'd start  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> by hooking up a remote starter button, and go from there.||||reg||12
Z||000002||polomeno||11-08-2000||11:16 PM||||Remote starter button?? how do i do that??<P>thanks <P>John||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||11-08-2000||11:31 PM||||Remote starter button (remote starter switch)is a piece of tune-up equipment, available  from parts stores, if you don't already have one.  It's just a heavy duty,  momentary-contact switch with two wires and aligator clips.  Hook one to the (small!) starter terminal; the other to the battery.  Make sure car is not in gear.<P>Briefly hit the button.  The starter should crank the engine.  That just tells you the starter and battery are good.  Usually, people use them for compression test, or to "bump" the engine into a desired position.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||polomeno||11-08-2000||11:34 PM||||The starter and battery are brand new.. the problem is in the neutral start switch.. this is what i need to trouble shoot or disable.. or work around.. anything  will do  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>thanks<P>John||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||11-09-2000||12:22 AM||||Wild stab number two...<P>If your car has cruise, control, there's more than one switch at the clutch pedal. <P>Don't suppose you shorted across the cruise control switch, rather than the neutral start switch? The neutral start has a big purple wire, and a yellow one, I think.||||reg||5
Z||000006||polomeno||11-09-2000||07:28 AM||||nope no cruise control on it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||11-09-2000||08:02 AM||||Wild stab number two (think there's a killer "O.J." joke lurking, but I'll pass):<P>Leave trans. in neutral.  Get a 12-volt test light.  Connect its clip to a decent ground.  Poke the probe into one terminal of the neutral start switch(NSS) connector - the yellow wire, I think.<P>Now turn the key to START position.  The test light should glow.  If not, try the other terminal of the NSS connector.  If this experiment is a total "no glow," then  something's wrong upstream of the NSS.      ||||reg||3
Z||000008||Indiana_resto_guy||11-09-2000||08:06 AM||||Check your starter connections and fuse link at the starter (may be loose). Check batery ground as well.||||reg||1
